Language, Self-Model, and Metacognition: 


A Theoretical–Computational Investigation of the Causal Role of Language in Self-Representation

Status: 

Final theoretical blueprint. Version 4.0

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Epistemic guardrail: This thesis does not claim that language creates phenomenal consciousness. 

It formalizes, simulates, and proposes to test how linguistic training transforms functional self-representation, metacognition, and narrative identity in an organism that already has a living body, interoception, and development. 

Phenomenal consciousness remains an unobserved latent variable P, and no functional measure is treated as direct evidence about P.

0. Preamble

This document is the theoretical core of the thesis. It is organized as follows. Section 1 fixes the epistemic framework and the taxonomy of language–consciousness relations. Section 2 develops the mathematical foundations: probability, Bayesian inference, information theory, dynamical systems, causal inference, psychometrics, and learning theory. Section 3 states and proves the formal theorems that constrain the architecture. Section 4 specifies the architecture. Section 5 specifies the algorithms. Section 6 gives the step-by-step implementation roadmap as future work. Sections 7–9 state contributions, limitations, and conclusions.

No empirical results are reported. No simulations have been run. No training has been performed. The document is a design specification for a theoretical–computational investigation.

