1. Epistemic Framework

1.1 Constructs

Four constructs are distinguished:

Symbol Construct Status
P Phenomenal consciousness Unobserved latent variable
S_t Self-model at time t Functional
M_t Metacognitive state Functional
R_t Narrative identity state Functional

Language L is decomposed into four components:

Symbol Component
L_c Conceptual–linguistic competence
L_i Inner speech
L_n Narrative language
L_e Emotion-word concepts

1.2 Taxonomy of Language–Consciousness Relations

Relation Claim Status in this thesis
Constitutive Language constitutes or is identical with consciousness Rejected as empirical claim
Causal Language causes consciousness to exist Not claimed; P is pre-existing
Enabling Language provides necessary conditions for certain forms of self-awareness Partially adopted for S/M/R; tested via mediation
Modulatory Language transforms functional self-representation and may modulate aspects of lived experience Primary thesis claim

The modulatory relation is the testable claim. The constitutive relation is a philosophical position, not an empirical hypothesis.

1.3 Three Models

· Model A — Language-specific causal contribution: linguistic training improves S or M beyond active control.
· Model B — General cognitive explanation: improvements are driven by attention, memory, social interaction, or general cognitive training.
· Model C — Language–organism interaction: effects of language depend on pre-existing interoception, inner speech, age, or developmental history.

1.4 Hypotheses

H1: Targeted linguistic training improves at least one pre-specified measure of metacognitive efficiency (log-transformed meta\text{-}d'/d' in perception and memory) or self-representation.

H2: The effect is moderated by baseline interoception, inner speech frequency, or developmental history.

H2a (strong inference): The training effect on self-representation is significantly smaller in low-inner-speech/anendophasic participants than in high-inner-speech participants if inner speech causally mediates the effect.

H3: Language alone is insufficient for normal human development; it operates on a biological, embodied system.

H4: Models A, B, and C can be distinguished by patterns of ITT effects, mediation, and interaction.

H5: Narrative coherence dimensions (context, chronology, theme) respond differentially to linguistic training, with theme (causal-motivational) showing the strongest language-specific effect.

H6: Metacognitive efficiency improvements, if present, generalize across at least two domains (perception and memory), as indexed by a positive cross-domain correlation.

