2. Mathematical Foundations

2.1 Probability and Measure-Theoretic Setup

Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}) be a probability space. The agent's state at time t is a random vector

Z_t = (X_t, I_t, L_t, S_t, M_t, R_t, A_t) \in \mathcal{Z}

with joint law \mathbb{P}_\theta parameterized by \theta \in \Theta. The generative model factorizes as

p_\theta(Z_{1:T}) = p(\theta) \prod_{t=1}^{T} p(X_t | S_t) \, p(I_t | S_t) \, p(L_t | S_t) \, p(S_t | S_{t-1}, L_t, I_t) \, p(R_t | R_{t-1}, S_t) \, p(A_t | S_t, M_t, R_t).

The self-model posterior is approximated variationally:

q_\phi(S_t | X_{\leq t}, I_{\leq t}, L_{\leq t}) \approx p_\theta(S_t | X_{\leq t}, I_{\leq t}, L_{\leq t}),

with \phi the variational parameters.

2.2 Bayesian Inference

Prior–likelihood–posterior. For a Gaussian self-model with prior precision \tau_0 = 1/\sigma_0^2 and likelihood precision \tau_L = 1/\sigma_L^2,

p(S_t | L_t) \propto \exp\!\left(-\frac{\tau_0}{2}(S_t - \mu_0)^2\right) \exp\!\left(-\frac{\tau_L}{2}(L_t - S_t)^2\right).

Variational free energy. For an approximate posterior q,

\mathcal{F}[q] = \mathbb{E}_q[\log q(S) - \log p(O, S)] = \mathrm{KL}(q(S) \| p(S | O)) - \log p(O).

Expected free energy. For a policy \pi,

G(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_{q(\cdot | \pi)}[\log q(S | \pi) - \log p(O, S | \pi)].

Action selection minimizes G(\pi).

2.3 Information Theory

Mutual information.

I(L; S \mid X, I) = \sum_{l,s} p(l,s \mid x,i) \log \frac{p(l,s \mid x,i)}{p(l \mid x,i) \, p(s \mid x,i)}.

Transfer entropy.

T_{L \to S} = I(S_{t+1}; L_t \mid S_t).

Chain rule for conditional mutual information.

I(X; Y, Z \mid W) = I(X; Y \mid W) + I(X; Z \mid W, Y).

2.4 Dynamical Systems and Control

Self-model dynamics:

S_{t+1} = f_\theta(S_t, L_t, I_t, X_t, A_t) + \omega_t, \qquad \omega_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\omega).

Narrative dynamics:

R_{t+1} = R_t + \eta \nabla_R C(R_t) + \nu_t,

where C(R) = [C_{\text{ctx}}(R), C_{\text{chron}}(R), C_{\text{theme}}(R)] follows the NaCCS three-dimension scheme (context, chronology, theme).

2.5 Causal Inference and Mediation

Potential outcomes. For treatment T \in \{0,1\} and outcome Y,

\text{ATE} = \mathbb{E}[Y(1) - Y(0)].

ITT regression.

Y_{\text{post}} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 T + \beta_2 Y_{\text{pre}} + \varepsilon.

Natural direct and indirect effects.

\text{NDE} = \mathbb{E}[Y(1, M(0)) - Y(0, M(0))], \qquad \text{NIE} = \mathbb{E}[Y(1, M(1)) - Y(1, M(0))].

Identification assumptions: consistency, positivity, ignorability of treatment, no unmeasured exposure–mediator confounding, no unmeasured mediator–outcome confounding, no exposure-induced mediator–outcome confounder.

Sensitivity analysis: parameterize the violation of mediator–outcome ignorability by \rho = \mathrm{corr}(\varepsilon_M, \varepsilon_Y) and report \rho^* where ACME(\rho^*) = 0.

2.6 Psychometric Theory

Reliability of a difference score. For two parallel measures X, Y with reliability \rho_{xx}, true-score correlation \rho_T, and observed correlation \rho_{XY},

\rho_{DD'} = \frac{\rho_{xx} - \rho_{XY}}{1 - \rho_{XY}}.

Equivalence testing (TOST). For margin \Delta, true difference \delta, and standard error \mathrm{SE},

\text{Power} \approx \Phi\!\left(\frac{\Delta - \delta}{\mathrm{SE}} - z_\alpha\right) + \Phi\!\left(\frac{\Delta + \delta}{\mathrm{SE}} - z_\alpha\right) - 1.

For \delta = 0, required sample size per group:

n \geq \frac{2\sigma^2 (z_\alpha + z_\beta)^2}{\Delta^2}.

Gatekeeping. Fixed-sequence gatekeeping orders hypotheses H_1, \ldots, H_k, tests H_1 at \alpha, and proceeds to H_2 only if H_1 is rejected, and so on. FWER \leq \alpha.

2.7 Learning Theory

Transformer attention.

\mathrm{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \mathrm{softmax}\!\left(\frac{QK^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right) V.

Meta-learning update.

\theta' = \theta - \alpha \nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\{T_i\}}(f_\theta).

Language composition.

z^L = f_\theta(v_{w_1}, \ldots, v_{w_n}), \qquad L_i = (z^L_1, \ldots, z^L_k).

