8. Limitations and Risks

8.1 P is unobservable. No functional measure in this thesis is evidence about P. The P/S distinction is a methodological guardrail, not an empirical hypothesis.

8.2 Language operationalization is a simplifying model. The L_c/L_i/L_n/L_e decomposition is not a complete taxonomy. Cross-module transfer may necessitate a composite training condition.

8.3 Causal inference depends on untestable assumptions. Sensitivity analysis (Theorem 6) quantifies but does not eliminate the risk of unmeasured confounding.

8.4 Simulation results may not generalize to humans. LLM agents lack interoception and embodied development. The simulation-to-human bridging uses ordinal predictions only (Algorithm 9), not a fixed attenuation threshold.

8.5 Active inference may be falsified. If Theorem 12's condition fails, the active inference component is removed and the architecture is relabeled.

8.6 The self-model trap is exploratory. No validated human analog exists. The trap is a simulation target, not a primary human outcome.

8.7 Domain specificity of metacognition is unresolved. The cross-domain correlation is a pre-specified outcome (H6), not an assumption.

8.8 Emotional granularity measurement is fragile. The ICC of idiographic categorical labels is undefined; the similarity-based method and within-person variability index are the primary and alternative measures.

8.9 Multiple primary outcomes inflate error. Gatekeeping (Algorithm 7) controls the FWER.

8.10 Equivalence claims require margins. Model B and Model C falsification use TOST with pre-registered margins (Algorithm 8).

8.11 Registered Reports may carry a citation penalty. A companion theoretical/computational paper is planned.

