9. Concluding Statement

Version 4.0 is the final theoretical blueprint. It integrates all validated proposals from the critical audit, resolves the three definitional inconsistencies (NaCCS dimensions, emotional granularity definition, self-reference vs. theme), and specifies the mathematics, proofs, architecture, algorithms, and implementation roadmap.

The thesis's core claim — that language transforms S, M, and R without creating P — is formalized through twelve theorems. The Kalman gain theorem identifies the precision parameter \gamma as bounded in (0,1). The Banach fixed point theorem establishes the existence of self-model attractors. The trap escape theorem gives the threshold for linguistic perturbation to escape suboptimal attractors. The transfer entropy decomposition separates the mediated and direct paths from language to self-model. The mediation identifiability theorem grounds the causal claim. The sensitivity bound quantifies robustness. The reliability-of-change theorem shows that high test–retest reliability of the level does not imply high reliability of the change. The gatekeeping theorem controls family-wise error. The equivalence testing theorem gives the power and sample size for falsification. The NaCCS separability theorem grounds the three-dimension measurement. The false-positive control theorem mandates a prior on the group difference. The active inference falsifiability theorem specifies the condition under which the active inference component is removed.

No empirical data have been collected. No code has been trained. No physical tests have been administered. The document is a theoretical specification for future work, with the implementation roadmap given in Section 6.

The next step is to execute Phase 1: symbolic verification of Theorems 1–12 and formalization of the state space and generative model. Phase 2 specifies the architecture, Phase 3 the simulation design, Phase 4 the measurement protocol, Phase 5 the pre-registration, and Phase 6 the future empirical phase. The thesis is theoretically complete and empirically ready.


Appendices: 

Appendix A — Mathematical Foundations

A.1 Measure-Theoretic Setup

Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}) be a probability space. The agent's state at time t is a random vector

Z_t = (X_t, I_t, L_t, S_t, M_t, R_t, A_t) \in \mathcal{Z},

with joint law \mathbb{P}_\theta parameterized by \theta \in \Theta. The generative model factorizes as

p_\theta(Z_{1:T}) = p(\theta) \prod_{t=1}^{T} p(X_t \mid S_t) \, p(I_t \mid S_t) \, p(L_t \mid S_t) \, p(S_t \mid S_{t-1}, L_t, I_t) \, p(R_t \mid R_{t-1}, S_t) \, p(A_t \mid S_t, M_t, R_t).

The self-model posterior is approximated variationally:

q_\phi(S_t \mid X_{\leq t}, I_{\leq t}, L_{\leq t}) \approx p_\theta(S_t \mid X_{\leq t}, I_{\leq t}, L_{\leq t}),

with \phi the variational parameters.

A.2 Bayesian Inference and Free Energy

For a Gaussian self-model with prior precision \tau_0 = 1/\sigma_0^2 and likelihood precision \tau_L = 1/\sigma_L^2,

p(S_t \mid L_t) \propto \exp\!\left(-\frac{\tau_0}{2}(S_t - \mu_0)^2\right) \exp\!\left(-\frac{\tau_L}{2}(L_t - S_t)^2\right).

The variational free energy is

\mathcal{F}[q] = \mathbb{E}_q[\log q(S) - \log p(O, S)] = \mathrm{KL}(q(S) \| p(S \mid O)) - \log p(O).

The expected free energy for a policy \pi is

G(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_{q(\cdot \mid \pi)}[\log q(S \mid \pi) - \log p(O, S \mid \pi)].

Action selection minimizes G(\pi).

A.3 Information-Theoretic Quantities

The conditional mutual information between language and self-model is

I(L; S \mid X, I) = \sum_{l,s} p(l,s \mid x,i) \log \frac{p(l,s \mid x,i)}{p(l \mid x,i) \, p(s \mid x,i)}.

The transfer entropy from language to self-model is

T_{L \to S} = I(S_{t+1}; L_t \mid S_t).

The chain rule for conditional mutual information gives

I(X; Y, Z \mid W) = I(X; Y \mid W) + I(X; Z \mid W, Y).

A.4 Dynamical Systems

Self-model dynamics:

S_{t+1} = f_\theta(S_t, L_t, I_t, X_t, A_t) + \omega_t, \qquad \omega_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_\omega).

Narrative dynamics:

R_{t+1} = R_t + \eta \nabla_R C(R_t) + \nu_t,

where C(R) = [C_{\text{ctx}}(R), C_{\text{chron}}(R), C_{\text{theme}}(R)] follows the NaCCS three-dimension scheme. The NaCCS context dimension evaluates the degree to which the narrative is oriented in time and place; the chronology dimension evaluates whether components are related along a timeline; the theme dimension evaluates how well the narrator maintains the topic and elaborates it using causal statements, personal interpretations, and evaluations.

A.5 Causal Inference

For treatment T \in \{0,1\} and outcome Y, the average treatment effect is

\text{ATE} = \mathbb{E}[Y(1) - Y(0)].

The ITT regression is

Y_{\text{post}} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 T + \beta_2 Y_{\text{pre}} + \varepsilon.

Natural direct and indirect effects:

\text{NDE} = \mathbb{E}[Y(1, M(0)) - Y(0, M(0))], \qquad \text{NIE} = \mathbb{E}[Y(1, M(1)) - Y(1, M(0))].

Identification requires consistency, positivity, ignorability of treatment, no unmeasured exposure–mediator confounding, no unmeasured mediator–outcome confounding, and no exposure-induced mediator–outcome confounder. The sensitivity parameter \rho denotes the correlation between the error terms of the mediator and outcome models, capturing the potential influence of an unobserved confounder that affects both. When \rho = 0, sequential ignorability holds; nonzero values imply violations. Sensitivity analysis identifies the values of \rho at which the ACME becomes zero or its confidence interval includes zero.

A.6 Psychometric Theory

The reliability of a difference score D = Y - X for parallel measures is

\rho_{DD'} = \frac{\rho_{xx} - \rho_{XY}}{1 - \rho_{XY}},

where \rho_{xx} is the reliability of each measure and \rho_{XY} is the observed correlation. The Reliable Change Index is

\text{RCI} = \frac{t_1 - t_0}{S_{\text{diff}}},

where S_{\text{diff}} = \sqrt{2(\text{SE})^2} and \text{SE} = \text{SD}\sqrt{1 - \hat{\text{rel}}}. Reliable change is defined as an individual score exceeding the cutoff RCI of 1.96.

For TOST with margin \Delta, true difference \delta, and standard error \text{SE}, the power is

\text{Power} \approx \Phi\!\left(\frac{\Delta - \delta}{\text{SE}} - z_\alpha\right) + \Phi\!\left(\frac{\Delta + \delta}{\text{SE}} - z_\alpha\right) - 1.

For \delta = 0, the required sample size per group satisfies

n \geq \frac{2\sigma^2 (z_\alpha + z_\beta)^2}{\Delta^2}.

A.7 Learning Theory

Transformer attention:

\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \text{softmax}\!\left(\frac{QK^\top}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right) V.

Meta-learning update:

\theta' = \theta - \alpha \nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_{\{T_i\}}(f_\theta).

Language composition:

z^L = f_\theta(v_{w_1}, \ldots, v_{w_n}), \qquad L_i = (z^L_1, \ldots, z^L_k).

